WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.663
Integer safety starts with understanding what a type can represent.

2
00:00:03.663 --> 00:00:08.059
An integer type is not just a small box for a number.

3
00:00:08.059 --> 00:00:12.821
It carries a range, a signedness rule, conversion behavior, and sometimes portability assumptions.

4
00:00:12.821 --> 00:00:18.682
When a value crosses an API boundary, those details can determine whether the result is meaningful.

5
00:00:18.682 --> 00:00:21.246
Signed and unsigned types serve different purposes.

6
00:00:21.246 --> 00:00:28.939
Signed types can represent values below zero, which may be appropriate for deltas, offsets, or states where negative values have meaning.

7
00:00:28.939 --> 00:00:34.434
Unsigned types represent nonnegative ranges, which can be useful for sizes and bit-oriented values.

8
00:00:34.434 --> 00:00:37.731
Problems appear when the code treats signedness as cosmetic.

9
00:00:37.731 --> 00:00:46.522
Fixed-width types such as std::uint32_t or std::int64_t are helpful when a format, protocol, file, or cross-platform boundary requires a specific

10
00:00:46.522 --> 00:00:46.888
representation.

11
00:00:46.888 --> 00:00:48.720
They make storage expectations clearer.

12
00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:56.779
They should still be paired with domain rules because the largest representable value is often much larger than the largest acceptable value.

13
00:00:56.779 --> 00:00:59.343
Other integer types have platform-dependent widths.

14
00:00:59.343 --> 00:01:04.838
Plain int, long, and related aliases can vary across compilers, operating systems, and build targets.

15
00:01:04.838 --> 00:01:09.600
Code that assumes one width may behave differently when moved to another environment.

16
00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:13.263
Portability-sensitive code should name representation and range expectations explicitly.

17
00:01:13.263 --> 00:01:17.659
size_t is the standard type for object sizes and many container sizes.

18
00:01:17.659 --> 00:01:23.886
It is useful and necessary, but it can create review challenges when mixed casually with signed values.

19
00:01:23.886 --> 00:01:27.916
A negative signed value converted to size_t no longer looks negative.

20
00:01:27.916 --> 00:01:32.678
Code should avoid casual mixing and should check range before crossing that boundary.

21
00:01:32.678 --> 00:01:36.707
The main design habit is to treat range limits as requirements.

22
00:01:36.707 --> 00:01:46.964
Ask what the value means, what unit it uses, what minimum and maximum are valid for the domain, and what should happen when the value is outside that

23
00:01:46.964 --> 00:01:47.331
range.

24
00:01:47.331 --> 00:01:51.360
The chosen type should support that contract instead of hiding it.
